WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.930
SPEAKER: When approaching
the architecture of your data

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.690
warehouse, it's important
to understand the constructs

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.550
of the services you'll use.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.910
So what are folders,
projects, and data sets,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.100
and how do they come
together to support

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.570
warehousing fundamentals like
security and cost management?

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.180
On today's episode of
"BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.670
we're talking about the
BigQuery resource model

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.600
and giving you our top tips for
structuring your own resources.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.528
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.780
Let's get started
by talking about how

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.250
BigQuery fits into the Google
Cloud resource hierarchy.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.940
An organization is the root node
of the Google Cloud resource

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:44.810
model.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.740
It represents a company,
probably the one you work for,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.440
and it's linked to a domain
through a Google Workspace

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.900
or Cloud Identity account.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.420
An organization
resource isn't required

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.680
to get started with BigQuery,
but it is recommended.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.220
With an organization, resources
like BigQuery data sets

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.710
or tables can be
centrally controlled

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.410
by organization
administrators, instead

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.460
of solely being owned by the
employee that creates them.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.310
Within an organization,
you create billing accounts

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.790
that specify who actually pays
for Google Cloud resources.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.790
You can also create
folders, which

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:26.720
offer an additional grouping
mechanism for resources.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.350
Folders often represent
business units or legal entities

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.160
within your company and make it
easier to organize and monitor

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.600
resource utilization.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.290
Folders also act as a
policy inheritance point.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.410
Access rules granted on a
folder are automatically

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.260
inherited by all
projects and folders

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.820
included in that folder.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.510
Now, let's go a
little bit deeper.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.060
Within an organization
or a folder,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.340
you can create
projects, which form

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.930
the basis for using BigQuery.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.840
Projects are tied to a
single billing account, which

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.750
means that if different teams
need to be billed individually

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.470
for their BigQuery usage, they
would need their own projects,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.150
and additional
billing accounts would

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.050
need to be created at
the organization level.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.280
Let's take a look at how this
resource hierarchy is reflected

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.860
in the Cloud Console,
where you interact with

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.650
and analyze your
data in BigQuery.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.500
In BigQuery, projects are
kind of like databases

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.190
in other systems.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.160
But here, you can
have separate projects

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.250
for storing and
analyzing your data.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.130
When opening up the console,
you can see your projects

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.340
on the left-hand side,
with the data stored nested

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.860
beneath each project name.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.860
All storage costs will be billed
to the associated projects

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.330
here.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.180
Even with data stored
across different projects,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.060
you can analyze it
together by joining tables

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.250
in a single query.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.100
At the top of the console,
you can see the project where

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.590
your query will actually run.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.230
All compute costs will
be billed to this project

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.030
and its associated
billing account.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:07.390
Speaking of billing, BigQuery
offers two different pricing

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.620
models.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.810
Projects with
on-demand pricing are

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.500
charged for the number of
bytes processed by each query.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.470
But with this model, there
are per-project limitations

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.990
and quotas that are
important to consider.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.680
The other option is
flat-rate pricing,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.060
where customers purchase compute
capacity to handle workloads.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.010
With this model,
reservations can

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.910
be assigned at the organization,
folder, or project levels

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.120
to handle jobs
within that resource.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.080
On-demand pricing is great for
getting started with BigQuery,

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.870
but as your consumption
grows, flat-rate offers

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.800
scalable compute with
predictable pricing.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.160
Great-- so now back
to the resource model.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.200
So far, we've
discussed resources

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.470
applicable to all Google
Cloud deployments,

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:01.270
but now, we'll focus on
resources specific to BigQuery.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.610
Within a project,
data sets are used

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.340
to house, organize,
and control access

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.780
to tables, views, routines,
and machine-learning models.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.320
They're similar to
schemas in other systems.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.980
When you create a data set, you
choose a geographic location

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.620
where your data will be stored.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.130
BigQuery offers a variety
of regions and multi-regions

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.930
for your data
residency requirements.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.230
However, you can't
query across data

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.430
that lives in
different locations,

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.190
so make sure to
plan accordingly.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.850
Data sets also store metadata.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.400
You can create
descriptions and labels,

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.350
which can be viewed
in the console

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.230
or used as tabs for monitoring.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.650
You can also find details
about each one of your BigQuery

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.790
resources by querying
the information schema.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.490
But data sets aren't the
only BigQuery resource

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.530
belonging to projects.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.080
Other concepts, like
connections and jobs,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.240
can be found here as well.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.000
We'll cover more detail on
these in the next few videos.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.720
Now that you understand
the top-level containers

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.180
of the BigQuery resource
model, let's talk

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.490
about some common ways users
structure their deployments.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.010
First off, some
BigQuery architects

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.520
choose to create a common
storage project that is queried

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.370
in other compute projects.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.190
In this case, a centralized
data platform team

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.100
can also create pipelines
in a storage project

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.690
to actually ingest data
from different sources.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.660
You can think of this storage
project as a data lake

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.330
or a storage repository that
holds a bunch of raw data.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.090
Then, each department can
have their own project

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.240
for querying the data,
saving results, and creating

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.400
aggregate views.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.040
You can think of these
projects as data marts,

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.380
or a repository
of summarized data

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.730
collected for analysis on a
unit within the organization,

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:00.010
for example, the
sales department.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.110
This structure is
especially useful

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.450
when departments want to
be individually billed

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:07.330
for their queries,
or if queries need

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.820
to be run in separate
projects to avoid

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.190
per project limitations.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.220
Another option is to
do the reverse, where

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.280
each department creates and
manages their own storage

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:21.150
project to house their raw data.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.330
Aggregate tables or
transformations of the raw data

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.820
are then stored in a
centralized data warehouse

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.440
project where analysts can
read and query the data.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.590
This structure allows for
project-level access controls

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.700
against raw data and offers
a simple data access layer

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.620
for connecting business
intelligence tools to BigQuery.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.590
Many BigQuery administrators
use some combination

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:47.630
of these two structures.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.760
When deciding on your
own architecture,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.590
you should be asking
questions like,

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:53.990
how often will analysts
be running queries

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.700
against these data sets?

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.740
How should storage
and compute be billed

00:06:57.740 --> 00:06:59.420
to our different departments?

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.720
And who should have
access to what data?

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.990
We hope this overview gets you
well on your way to designing

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.180
your BigQuery architecture.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.040
For more guidance
on getting started

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.830
with BigQuery or the
BigQuery resource model,

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.340
check out the links below.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.120
Thanks for joining us today
for "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.510
Watch out for the next episode,
and remember, stay curious.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.560
